WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:30.232 --> 00:01:31.065
- Hello everybody,

00:01:31.065 --> 00:01:32.230
it's Gary Veynerchuk

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:37.230
and this is episode 326
of the Ask Gary Vee Show.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:42.450
I'm super excited to have
Tilman Fertitta here with me.

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.320
I'm gonna let him introduce himself.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:47.620
But we are taking
questions on Facebook today

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.910
and so let's just get right into it.

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:51.380
Tilman thank you for being on.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:53.940
- Hey it's exciting to be here, Gary.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.312
Everybody wants to come and
be on the Gary Vee show.

00:01:56.312 --> 00:01:57.145
(laughter)

00:01:57.145 --> 00:01:57.978
- [Gary] I appreciate that.

00:01:57.978 --> 00:02:00.620
So Tilman for the group
that's watching right now

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.670
or listening that
doesn't know who you are,

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.620
I'm a big fan of the origin stories.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.080
So why don't you tell everybody

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:10.010
A, how you currently position yourself

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:12.309
because one thing I
adore about you from afar

00:02:12.309 --> 00:02:15.260
is you got your hands in
a lot of different stuff.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:17.250
It's that kind of entrepreneurial mindset

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.240
that I can attest to that.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:20.820
I understand it.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.748
So when somebody neat sits down with you

00:02:24.748 --> 00:02:26.410
and doesn't know who you are,

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:27.660
they're like, what do you do?

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.141
How do you answer it?

00:02:29.141 --> 00:02:31.700
- (laughs) That's really funny.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:33.200
That doesn't happen that often.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.360
But when it does I just say,

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.910
you know I'm just trying
to make a few dollars.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:39.520
I have a few little businesses

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.920
and my main business is a
company called Landry's.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:44.420
It's about 600 restaurants.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:47.420
And Landry's owns everything from

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:52.450
Mastro's to Martin's to
Rainforest to Bubba Gump

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:55.840
to Saltgrass to Oceanaire to Dos Caminos

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.560
to Bill's Burgers and on and on and on.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:02:59.631
There's like 50 brands.

00:02:59.631 --> 00:03:00.464
- [Gary] How many?

00:03:00.464 --> 00:03:01.297
5-0.

00:03:01.297 --> 00:03:02.340
- Yeah about 50 brands

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:04.970
and over 500 restaurants.

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:07.540
And then the five Golden
Nugget casinos are mine.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:12.540
And then aquariums,
amusement parks, Catch,

00:03:12.762 --> 00:03:15.890
and then I have this little sports team

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:17.220
called the Houston Rockets.

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:20.223
And I do that on the
side when I'm not busy.

00:03:21.861 --> 00:03:24.693
- (laughs) So let's take
it all the way back.

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:26.760
Where were you born?

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:28.105
What kind of kid were you?

00:03:28.105 --> 00:03:31.050
- (Tilman) I was born in Galveston, Texas.

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:32.270
It's a BOI.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:36.540
And I was never the
smartest kid in the class.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.350
I can honestly tell you that.

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:41.530
And I can remember a
teacher telling me once,

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:43.937
you know Mr. Fertitta we
know you have leadership

00:03:43.937 --> 00:03:48.937
abilities but we wish you would
lead in the right direction.

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:52.053
- [Gary] Look at this.

00:03:52.053 --> 00:03:53.624
I don't know how bad you were at school,

00:03:53.624 --> 00:03:56.070
but this is probably my pride and joy,

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:57.410
my report card.

00:03:57.410 --> 00:03:59.610
It's pretty fuckin' ugly.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:03.340
It is a doozy.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:05.060
As you're getting your
glasses why don't I,

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:05.893
oh there you go.

00:04:05.893 --> 00:04:07.820
- [Tilman] No I've got
to see these grades.

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:09.100
See who was worst,

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:09.933
me or you.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:13.794
This looks like one of
mine in third grade.

00:04:13.794 --> 00:04:15.793
- [Gary] (laughs) This is my high school.

00:04:15.793 --> 00:04:16.626
- You know what,

00:04:16.626 --> 00:04:18.610
I am going to find...

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:20.740
- [Gary] Look at my class rank.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:22.590
Look down there. 240 all
the way at the bottom.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:25.230
243 out of 254.

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:27.770
There were only 11 kids in my school

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:29.090
that were worse than me.

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:32.093
And there's no shot any
of the 11 are alive.

00:04:33.289 --> 00:04:34.850
- (Tilman) That is so good.

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:38.170
I just love this because
I wasn't far behind.

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:41.300
And that's what's
amazing is I've talked to

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:43.260
entrepreneurs,

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:45.120
we're given a different ability

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:48.140
and it's a god-given ability and we just

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:49.040
know how to do it.

00:04:50.625 --> 00:04:53.350
- [Gary] Knowing that
you've been around sport,

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:56.050
do you view entrepreneurs
like athletes that way?

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:58.740
Like James Hardin was given a gift.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:00.650
That's obvious to a lot of people.

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:03.430
It's a little less obvious
where you're going with this

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:04.730
on the entrepreneurial kick

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:06.630
but I couldn't agree with you more.

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.130
- Gary I talk about it all the time.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:09.560
I talk about it in this book

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:12.330
that God gave us all an ability

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:13.163
and he decided,

00:05:13.163 --> 00:05:16.000
Tilman I'm not gonna make you a musician.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.897
'Cause I took guitar
lessons for four years

00:05:17.897 --> 00:05:19.780
and I still couldn't play a chord.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:23.410
I'm not gonna let you win a Grammy,

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:24.830
I'm not gonna let you win an Emmy.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:26.300
We're sure not gonna ever let you

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:29.320
be an NBA all star or be
a professional athlete.

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:30.720
But you know what we're gonna do?

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:32.040
We're gonna give you a good little

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.210
business brain where you
know how to make money.

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:36.470
And I realized that when I
was in elementary school.

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:38.070
I would carry my business around.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:42.740
I wouldn't read a comic
book or do anything.

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:44.880
I was selling candy and
anything else I could

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:46.500
buy and sell in junior high.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.200
In high school I was already
trading the stock market.

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:54.200
In college I was already booking bets.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:55.033
- I get it.

00:05:56.448 --> 00:05:57.994
What did your parents do?

00:05:57.994 --> 00:06:00.240
- [Tilman] My dad owned
a seafood restaurant

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.910
when I was growing up
and was an entrepreneur.

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:04.564
And there's lots of entrepreneurs in our

00:06:04.564 --> 00:06:08.220
family genetic pool on both sides.

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:09.053
- [Gary] That's cool.

00:06:09.053 --> 00:06:12.120
- And so definitely it's a gene.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:13.030
- [Gary] Yes no question.

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:14.010
- Without a doubt.

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:15.060
- [Gary] No question.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:17.990
- And but I can remember my dad though

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:20.120
after I had my fifth restaurant

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.180
and I was making a couple
million dollars a year

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:24.572
saying why do you want to do anymore?

00:06:24.572 --> 00:06:25.405
- [Gary] You made it.

00:06:25.405 --> 00:06:26.238
- You're making more money,

00:06:26.238 --> 00:06:27.367
you have made it.

00:06:27.367 --> 00:06:31.090
- By the way I helped build
my dad's one wine store

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:34.970
into a national e-commerce
player in the late 90's.

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:37.830
And everybody in my high
school and my friend group

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:41.480
said I'd made it and I remember
when they would say that

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:43.950
to me at 28, 29, 30, and I left at 34.

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:45.320
It was a family business,

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:47.410
I built it from a three to
a 60 million dollar business

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:49.410
and then I left to start at zero

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:50.880
'cause it was my dad's business.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:51.850
And that's what I wanted to do.

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:52.880
I wanted to give back to my dad.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:54.260
I was born in the Soviet Union.

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:57.780
I was very proud and grateful
for what my parents did for me

00:06:57.780 --> 00:06:59.480
but at 29, 30, 31,

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:01.630
my friends were like you made it.

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:02.640
'Cause they would read the articles,

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:04.220
they would hear the numbers.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:05.260
And I used to laugh,

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:06.820
I'm like they don't even
know that I'm about to

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:08.610
re-start from zero.

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:09.443
- [Tilman] Totally.

00:07:09.443 --> 00:07:11.620
- I made it. Like by the
way I feel like I haven't

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:13.190
fuckin' started right now.

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:14.340
- [Tilman] I get up every day

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:15.510
and it's kind of funny.

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:16.640
And I'm a little older than you,

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:18.663
and it's just sport.
- [Gary] It is just sport.

00:07:18.663 --> 00:07:21.540
- And when I was 21 I
won my first Cadillac

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:23.180
selling Shaklee Vitamins.

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:26.380
I said when I'm 35 I'm
gonna buy my first jet.

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:27.310
I did.

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:28.776
And on and on and on.

00:07:28.776 --> 00:07:29.609
- [Gary] Let me ask you a question.

00:07:29.609 --> 00:07:32.260
Have you thought about tangible things?

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:33.832
Like for example,

00:07:33.832 --> 00:07:36.610
it doesn't come natural to this day.

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:38.770
I want to buy the New York Jets.

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:41.690
So you've accomplished
something I want to do, right?

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:44.800
But I don't want anything else in between.

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:46.180
But were you somebody who wanted stuff

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:48.910
like Cadillacs, jets, you like that shit?

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:49.890
Or you thought it meant something?

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:51.010
I'm just curious how your brain worked.

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:52.430
- [Tilman] No no, totally.

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:56.140
I mean I had in my brain
that this is what I want.

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:58.660
And I'm gonna try to get
from point a to point b

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:00.330
however I have to do it.

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:01.760
And I hope you're not in
the way 'cause I have to

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:02.904
run over your ass.

00:08:02.904 --> 00:08:03.737
- [Gary] I understand.

00:08:03.737 --> 00:08:07.760
- But I wanted the
Cadillac, I wanted the...

00:08:09.634 --> 00:08:11.590
- [Gary] Can I ask you a deep question?

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:12.980
- [Tilman] Deep.

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:15.600
- Do you think you wanted
that to show the people

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:17.520
that thought you were not going to win

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.080
that you were winning as an indicator?

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:19.980
I think about this a lot.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:24.980
I don't have this great
need to show anybody,

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:27.480
it's unbelievable to me how much I don't

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:31.030
give a fuck if they know
if I'm winning or not.

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:33.040
I'm just obsessed with the process.

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:35.540
However there are many
other things I have.

00:08:35.540 --> 00:08:39.160
I'm just always curious with
ones that go along the way,

00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:41.750
'cause we're all different in our nuances.

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:45.323
I'm always fascinated by what
did those things represent?

00:08:46.993 --> 00:08:48.350
Some people just like stuff.

00:08:48.350 --> 00:08:51.480
I'm fascinated by people
who love a car or a jet.

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:52.313
They understand it,

00:08:52.313 --> 00:08:53.570
they're like it's got the horse power.

00:08:53.570 --> 00:08:55.130
I'm like what the fuck, right?

00:08:55.130 --> 00:08:57.180
And there's other people
who need it to be like,

00:08:57.180 --> 00:08:59.790
I'm gonna fuckin' show Susan
Thompson in third grade

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:01.410
who didn't go to the dance with me

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:02.950
that she fucked up.

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:06.210
- No I think it was more of the luxury.

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:09.471
And how I wanted to live.

00:09:09.471 --> 00:09:12.730
I never had that mentor
and I also never had that

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:14.560
I need to show you so.

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:16.567
It was more in competitiveness with myself

00:09:16.567 --> 00:09:18.380
and how I wanted to live.

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:21.863
And I'm proud that I've never
gone through a TSA line.

00:09:22.885 --> 00:09:25.710
- [Gary] (laughs) And
what's crazy about that,

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:27.580
this is what I love
about this shit Tilman.

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:29.400
And on the flip side,

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:33.900
I put out so much content
of me being at the airport.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:36.620
Like I on the flip side love that I limit

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:38.510
my private travel.

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:40.560
And we're doing the same thing.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:42.250
We're both putting something that we've

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:45.850
subjectively decided is
important on a pedestal.

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:46.770
- [Tilman] No 100%.

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:47.603
- Yeah.

00:09:47.603 --> 00:09:48.436
- [Tilman] It's interesting.

00:09:48.436 --> 00:09:49.610
- Yeah I mean everyday I'm like,

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:50.710
I'm such a fuckin' idiot.

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:52.170
Time is the only asset

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:55.740
and I'm fucking putzing
around on fuckin' commercial

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:56.573
but then I'm like,

00:09:56.573 --> 00:09:58.270
fuck so many people are watching.

00:09:58.270 --> 00:10:02.170
They get so much visuals around
private and all this stuff

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:04.390
and I'm still comfortable
like this I like it

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:05.530
and it's a very interesting thing

00:10:05.530 --> 00:10:07.280
that I play ping-pong in my head.

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:08.570
- [Tilman] No 'cause even so many

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:10.367
close friends of mine
have always said to me,

00:10:10.367 --> 00:10:13.060
are you ever gonna take
me on one of your jets?

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:14.540
I've known you for all these years.

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:16.640
I don't do anything to show off.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:17.550
It is strictly...

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:18.383
- [Gary] I believe you.

00:10:18.383 --> 00:10:19.216
- This is the way I wanna live.

00:10:19.216 --> 00:10:20.049
- [Gary] I believe you.

00:10:20.049 --> 00:10:21.503
- It's all about how you wanna live.

00:10:22.424 --> 00:10:26.584
- What's the least efficient thing you do

00:10:26.584 --> 00:10:28.940
that is around how you wanna live?

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:30.720
Like what's something that's silly?

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:33.160
Like I think me flying
commercial is silly.

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:34.110
It's not a good use of my time.

00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:36.430
But it's how I want to
live so I understand it.

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:38.090
What is something funny that you do

00:10:38.090 --> 00:10:40.080
that doesn't make sense?

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:45.080
- Probably I love being
in Monte Carlo on my yacht

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:49.230
but going shopping at Zara

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:50.800
and coming back to my boat

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:54.130
and laying out to all of my crew that,

00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:56.590
look at all these jackets I just bought,

00:10:56.590 --> 00:11:00.200
how gorgeous they are
for 18.99 euros each,

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:03.420
that I had six gorgeous
jackets that I only spent

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:06.910
130 euros on and I was proud of myself

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:08.800
I couldn't wait to show my crew.

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:10.320
That's what I'm proud of,

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:12.360
even though I like these nice things,

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:14.209
I love to make a deal and

00:11:14.209 --> 00:11:16.690
love to spend money on what I like.

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:18.597
I'll spend money on the big things

00:11:18.597 --> 00:11:21.120
but I watch how I spend
it on the little things.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:22.330
- I understand.

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:23.163
I really do.

00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:26.160
So how did your career go?

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:28.023
You sold some stuff,

00:11:28.023 --> 00:11:28.856
you won a Cadillac,

00:11:28.856 --> 00:11:29.689
you were a good salesman,

00:11:29.689 --> 00:11:32.032
gift of gab, charisma.

00:11:32.032 --> 00:11:33.490
Conviction.

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:34.323
You know, right?

00:11:34.323 --> 00:11:35.156
Determination.

00:11:35.156 --> 00:11:36.123
I understand all that shit.

00:11:37.370 --> 00:11:39.860
Because your dad had a seafood restaurant,

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:42.040
did you work in that
seafood restaurant as a kid?

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:42.910
- [Tilman] Yeah 100%.

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:44.050
- Learned the craft.

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:47.410
- [Tilman] So I learned how
the restaurant business worked.

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:49.010
Then you go to school,

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:53.283
you get out and I got into
the development business.

00:11:53.283 --> 00:11:54.550
I was building homes.

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:56.250
Got into the video game business,

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:57.610
the Pac-Man, Asteroids.

00:11:57.610 --> 00:11:58.990
- [Gary] Talk to me about that.

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:00.180
- I just timed it right.

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:01.900
- [Gary] No, no, I mean I don't know this.

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:02.970
I don't know a lot about anything.

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:04.860
So that's why I love having these things.

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:05.857
What did you do with that?

00:12:05.857 --> 00:12:09.320
- [Tilman] Back in the
80's you had Asteroids

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:11.970
that came out and it was
the first real video game.

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:16.310
And then it became Defender
and Pac-Man and all of those.

00:12:16.310 --> 00:12:18.870
Well I saw that the world was changing

00:12:18.870 --> 00:12:21.440
and so I went out and
bought these machines

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:23.270
and started putting them
in restaurants around.

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:27.440
And all of the sudden I
look up and I'm making

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:29.964
hundreds of thousands of dollars in cash.

00:12:29.964 --> 00:12:33.090
- [Gary] Are you literally
driving your Cadillac around

00:12:33.090 --> 00:12:34.830
grabbing buckets of fucking quarters?

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:37.410
- [Tilman] No but I went
out probably in the first,

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:38.430
I can't remember.

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:40.340
But no eventually we
had few trucks doing it.

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:41.173
- [Gary] Of course.

00:12:41.173 --> 00:12:42.380
- And all I remember is carrying all these

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:45.919
big bags of quarters and all I know is

00:12:45.919 --> 00:12:47.770
I was in high cotton.

00:12:47.770 --> 00:12:49.920
I was feeling really good about myself.

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:51.910
And did that for a few years

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:53.990
which gave me the opportunity to start

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:56.210
building homes and little shopping centers

00:12:56.210 --> 00:12:59.710
and then I built my first
hotel when I was 26 years old

00:12:59.710 --> 00:13:02.090
in my hometown which I still own today.

00:13:02.090 --> 00:13:02.958
- [Gary] That's cool.

00:13:02.958 --> 00:13:04.930
- And that's kind of the story.

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:06.690
Then the development business came apart,

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:09.730
all the banks failed in the late 80's.

00:13:09.730 --> 00:13:12.450
This is a great story and I
talk about it in the book.

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:15.080
I had loans at nine different banks

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:16.800
in the late 80's in Texas.

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:20.448
And this crisis was as bad
as the '08, '09 crisis.

00:13:20.448 --> 00:13:21.330
- [Gary] Yeah '87,

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:22.350
Black Friday was horrible.

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:23.200
- [Tilman] That was the,

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:24.883
the world came apart.

00:13:26.270 --> 00:13:31.270
Every bank that I did business
with failed before I did.

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:33.790
And so this two million worth of loans,

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:36.510
which was a bunch of money in the 80's,

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:37.810
there was no money to pay.

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:39.680
All of the sudden you
have to make a payment

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:41.490
and there's nobody to make a payment to.

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:42.760
The bank is gone.

00:13:42.760 --> 00:13:46.760
The FDIC has shut them
mother fuckers down, okay?

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:47.750
- [Gary] Tilman real quick,

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:48.890
'cause I want you to go back point,

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:51.610
please tell the kids here
how much two million dollars,

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:53.560
give me a number of the way you feel

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:55.250
two million dollars in '87,

00:13:55.250 --> 00:13:56.640
what's that equivalent to right now?

00:13:56.640 --> 00:13:58.498
- I would probably say 20 million.

00:13:58.498 --> 00:14:00.493
You know it would be
like having 20 million.

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:02.960
- [Gary] I apologize,

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:03.870
I always tell my audience,

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:06.390
I'm like my trendsetting
moment in my career

00:14:06.390 --> 00:14:07.720
was when I was right about YouTube

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:09.210
but I didn't invest in it.

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:11.120
And then I read when YouTube sold for

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:13.490
$1.7 billion to Google,

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:14.610
I don't know if you remember this

00:14:14.610 --> 00:14:15.850
or anybody else in the room,

00:14:15.850 --> 00:14:17.473
that sounded like a trillion.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:21.190
Now companies that don't make any money

00:14:21.190 --> 00:14:22.460
are worth a billion.

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:23.493
But at the time,

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:25.803
this is only 2007,

00:14:25.803 --> 00:14:28.520
1.7 billion?

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:29.652
It shook me.

00:14:29.652 --> 00:14:31.190
- [Tilman] It's a crazy number.

00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:32.835
And I'll give you an example.

00:14:32.835 --> 00:14:36.160
So basically what happened was

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:38.200
I got a reprieve from the governor, okay?

00:14:38.200 --> 00:14:40.360
And so all of the sudden I had five years

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:41.980
to keep growing my business before

00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:44.040
the FDIC called me and said,

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:46.180
Mr. Fertitta we have some loans here

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:48.120
we would like to talk to you about.

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:50.300
But by then I'd opened up
a bunch of restaurants,

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:51.310
made a bunch of money.

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:54.710
I walked in and the paid
the FDIC $2 million.

00:14:54.710 --> 00:14:56.190
They waived all the interest.

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:58.270
And they said you are the
only person to pay us in full.

00:14:58.270 --> 00:15:02.630
But I was rollin' then
and so things were good.

00:15:02.630 --> 00:15:04.570
Do you want to wait til that thing goes?

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:05.403
- [Gary] Keep going.

00:15:05.403 --> 00:15:06.640
- Doesn't bother you.

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:07.473
- [Gary] Keep going.

00:15:07.473 --> 00:15:08.306
- You know what's so,

00:15:08.306 --> 00:15:12.250
in 1982 I bought into the
Rockets for $200,000.00.

00:15:12.250 --> 00:15:13.200
- [Gary] Is that true?

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:14.033
I didn't know that.

00:15:14.033 --> 00:15:17.750
- We paid $10 million for
the Houston Rockets in 1982.

00:15:17.750 --> 00:15:19.320
- [Gary] You got 20% for $2 million?

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:21.223
- No no just the small percentage.

00:15:21.223 --> 00:15:22.056
- [Gary] I understand,

00:15:22.056 --> 00:15:23.440
one more time 'cause I didn't hear it.

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:24.920
- [Tilman] $200,000.00,

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:26.750
20% would have been 2 million.

00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:28.590
So I think we also had some debt on it.

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:30.770
I think I owned three or four percent.

00:15:30.770 --> 00:15:31.810
Whatever it was.

00:15:31.810 --> 00:15:35.680
Okay so I'm a partner in
the Rockets for 10 years.

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:37.750
The owner decides to sell.

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:39.330
The general partner says,

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:42.000
this is becoming a billionaire's game.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:44.750
Well this is in 1992 and I'm only worth

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:47.220
about 10, 15, million dollars.

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:50.010
Don't get me wrong
that's a bunch of money.

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:54.140
I didn't have the ability
to buy the team by myself.

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:56.640
So I had a partner and we tried to buy it

00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:00.070
and I got outbid by a New
Yorker, Les Alexander.

00:16:00.070 --> 00:16:02.520
He paid $80 million for this team.

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:04.930
So in 10 years the team
went from being worth

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:07.840
10 million to 80 million, okay?

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:09.960
Now here we go.

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:11.140
25 years later...

00:16:11.140 --> 00:16:12.806
- [Gary] Before you finish,
I just want to remind people,

00:16:12.806 --> 00:16:13.930
they've heard me say it a lot,

00:16:13.930 --> 00:16:17.823
the 1982 NBA Finals in
America was on tape delay.

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:20.200
- [Tilman] It was nothing.

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:23.080
- The league was nothing.

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:25.510
The way you guys think
about hockey right now,

00:16:25.510 --> 00:16:26.967
this was below that.

00:16:26.967 --> 00:16:28.120
And it's important,

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:29.730
and I'll tell you why it's important.

00:16:29.730 --> 00:16:31.650
And the reason I jumped in twice now,

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:34.430
the 2 million, the 20, the league,

00:16:34.430 --> 00:16:37.010
you and I have a similarity
as I'm listening.

00:16:37.010 --> 00:16:38.200
It's just arbitrage.

00:16:38.200 --> 00:16:42.640
It's seeing things just enough
time before somebody else

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:45.240
but then having the
conviction and the gumption

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:46.250
to fuckin' do something.

00:16:46.250 --> 00:16:48.364
- 100%.

00:16:48.364 --> 00:16:49.880
So think about this,

00:16:49.880 --> 00:16:54.088
in 1992 I get outbid for 80 million.

00:16:54.088 --> 00:16:57.460
25 years later I don't get the team

00:16:57.460 --> 00:16:59.080
so I'm building my company.

00:16:59.080 --> 00:16:59.970
So I build,

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:03.990
and that 25 years I grow my
net worth from 10 million

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:07.060
to almost 5 billion, okay?

00:17:07.060 --> 00:17:07.893
- [Gary] That's good.

00:17:07.893 --> 00:17:11.363
- The Rockets go up for
sale again 25 years later.

00:17:12.859 --> 00:17:14.550
I come in with the highest bid,

00:17:14.550 --> 00:17:15.383
I was right there.

00:17:15.383 --> 00:17:18.970
Most ever paid for a sports
franchise, 2.2 billion.

00:17:18.970 --> 00:17:21.550
So in 25 years the value of that team goes

00:17:21.550 --> 00:17:23.970
from 80 million to 2.2 billion.

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:25.060
And that's real dollars.

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:27.150
This isn't Monopoly money, okay?

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:28.500
You wire the money.

00:17:28.500 --> 00:17:30.340
You pay for it.

00:17:30.340 --> 00:17:34.420
- Tilman, were you a big basketball fan?

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:36.680
- This is so unbelievable.

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:39.750
I sit in the same seats today

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:42.280
that I sat in for the last 25 years,

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:43.113
center court.

00:17:45.510 --> 00:17:46.343
This is so funny,

00:17:46.343 --> 00:17:47.740
after I bought the team

00:17:47.740 --> 00:17:51.160
I'm one of the 30 longest
season ticket holders

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:52.100
of the Rockets.

00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:52.933
- [Gary] I love you.

00:17:52.933 --> 00:17:54.867
- [Tilman] Isn't that amazing?
- Not only is it amazing,

00:17:54.867 --> 00:17:55.700
it's literally gonna be

00:17:55.700 --> 00:17:58.490
I'm gonna tell this story in 31 years.

00:17:58.490 --> 00:17:59.323
I'm gonna clip this.

00:17:59.323 --> 00:18:00.540
You know how to clip old shit?

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:01.510
I'm gonna clip this and be like,

00:18:01.510 --> 00:18:02.360
I was sitting in this seat,

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:03.670
now I'm here and some new person,

00:18:03.670 --> 00:18:04.800
she's sitting in that seat.

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:06.450
I fuckin' understand.

00:18:06.450 --> 00:18:08.868
I would kill my children
for the New York Jets.

00:18:08.868 --> 00:18:11.118
(laughter)

00:18:12.530 --> 00:18:13.380
- You know what?

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:15.020
I would sell my children,

00:18:15.020 --> 00:18:16.300
I won't kill 'em,

00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:18.733
for the Houston Texans or
the Dallas Cowboys (laughs).

00:18:18.733 --> 00:18:20.483
- [Gary] You like both those teams?

00:18:21.520 --> 00:18:22.742
You want to own an NFL team,

00:18:22.742 --> 00:18:23.575
is that what you're saying?

00:18:23.575 --> 00:18:25.619
- I would love to own an NFL team.

00:18:25.619 --> 00:18:27.700
- [Gary] As a young kid,

00:18:27.700 --> 00:18:29.220
who was your favorite team?

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:30.941
- You know what's kind of funny is...

00:18:30.941 --> 00:18:32.610
- [Gary] 'Cause baseball was religion

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:33.557
when you were a kid.

00:18:33.557 --> 00:18:35.960
- And I was the biggest
Astros buddy there was.

00:18:35.960 --> 00:18:37.790
There was Astros all over.

00:18:37.790 --> 00:18:39.648
But what happened was,

00:18:39.648 --> 00:18:42.582
you young guys don't know this but...

00:18:42.582 --> 00:18:43.840
- [Gary] They don't know shit.

00:18:43.840 --> 00:18:45.046
- Ya'll don't know shit.

00:18:45.046 --> 00:18:46.259
(laughter)

00:18:46.259 --> 00:18:48.009
Ya'll don't know how bad we had it.

00:18:48.964 --> 00:18:51.390
You couldn't even watch
the NFL team in Houston

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:52.540
and you lived in Galveston

00:18:52.540 --> 00:18:55.080
'cause it was the 50 mile blackout rule.

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:58.148
'Cause they wanted you
to come to the game.

00:18:58.148 --> 00:18:59.610
- If they didn't sell out,

00:18:59.610 --> 00:19:01.846
they left you out.

00:19:01.846 --> 00:19:04.870
That's how the Raiders
and Cowboys and Steelers

00:19:04.870 --> 00:19:07.110
became all people's fans

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:08.220
because when they would black you out,

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:09.949
they'd show you one of those three teams.

00:19:09.949 --> 00:19:11.525
- [Tilman] Because Dallas was

00:19:11.525 --> 00:19:14.790
the next closest team

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:16.380
and remember this,

00:19:16.380 --> 00:19:18.040
when do you become a sports fan?

00:19:18.040 --> 00:19:19.430
Nine, ten years old.

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:20.690
So I'm nine, ten years old,

00:19:20.690 --> 00:19:22.140
that's when the Cowboys start playing the

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:24.630
Green Bay Packers and the Ice Bowls,

00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:25.910
and I'm watching them because

00:19:25.910 --> 00:19:26.943
the Houston Oilers aren't on TV.

00:19:26.943 --> 00:19:28.810
- [Gary] Tilman, as somebody
who's in a market with

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:30.890
two other basketball teams
in the state of Texas

00:19:30.890 --> 00:19:33.032
even though it's a big state,

00:19:33.032 --> 00:19:35.960
I have an idea for you that I'm gonna do

00:19:35.960 --> 00:19:37.510
when I buy the Jets.

00:19:37.510 --> 00:19:38.850
When I buy the Jets,

00:19:38.850 --> 00:19:42.020
because I hate the Giants
more than anything,

00:19:42.020 --> 00:19:43.030
well not more than the Patriots,

00:19:43.030 --> 00:19:46.050
but I hate the Giants, right?

00:19:46.050 --> 00:19:47.320
When I buy the Jets,

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:50.490
my core marketing strategy
to win the Tri-state area

00:19:50.490 --> 00:19:53.860
is on every single person's sixth birthday

00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:56.200
I'm gonna send them a Jet's jersey

00:19:56.200 --> 00:19:57.970
and personalized messages.

00:19:57.970 --> 00:20:00.960
I think you should start
sending six year olds

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:03.580
jerseys in San Antonio and Dallas

00:20:03.580 --> 00:20:05.603
of Rocket players.
- [Tilman] Why in the hell

00:20:05.603 --> 00:20:06.780
did you tell me this on air

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:07.977
so I couldn't have said that

00:20:07.977 --> 00:20:10.293
I'm the smart mother fucker
that came up with this?

00:20:10.293 --> 00:20:11.126
(laughter)

00:20:11.126 --> 00:20:11.959
Let me be smart please?

00:20:11.959 --> 00:20:14.000
- Tilman this is the greatest
strategy I came up with.

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:15.500
Because I have good ideas,

00:20:15.500 --> 00:20:17.670
and because I can't execute all of them,

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:20.913
I put them out for free
so that I can own the IP.

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:23.030
- Interesting

00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:23.863
- [Gary] Right?

00:20:23.863 --> 00:20:24.896
- Interesting, 100%.

00:20:24.896 --> 00:20:26.830
- [Gary] Think about all the smart shit,

00:20:26.830 --> 00:20:28.370
just getting a sense of you right now.

00:20:28.370 --> 00:20:30.320
By the way I'm gonna say this publicly,

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:34.446
I'm not joking I had
no clue you owned the,

00:20:34.446 --> 00:20:36.530
I don't know much about a lot of stuff.

00:20:36.530 --> 00:20:38.790
I just spend 100% of my
time on the consumer.

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:40.110
I just don't know a lot of stuff.

00:20:40.110 --> 00:20:42.500
I just know what customers
are doing at all times.

00:20:42.500 --> 00:20:44.200
That's why I don't know
much of other stuff.

00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:45.450
That takes a lot of time.

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:48.760
So this gets booked through email,

00:20:48.760 --> 00:20:50.280
Matt put us together.

00:20:50.280 --> 00:20:52.010
But I know of you of course.

00:20:52.010 --> 00:20:54.900
And over the last week I've been with some

00:20:54.900 --> 00:20:58.493
fancy different people
like movers and shakers.

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:02.470
The reason I was so excited
to come in here today

00:21:02.470 --> 00:21:06.210
is I basically live my life for people

00:21:06.210 --> 00:21:08.310
to talk about me behind my back

00:21:08.310 --> 00:21:10.410
the way the four people that
I spoke to in the last week

00:21:10.410 --> 00:21:12.491
about you talked about you.

00:21:12.491 --> 00:21:13.840
- [Tilman] Interesting.

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:15.660
That is interesting.

00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:17.074
I don't know if it was good or bad.

00:21:17.074 --> 00:21:18.115
- [Gary] It was very good.

00:21:18.115 --> 00:21:23.115
It was very good because
what was interesting is

00:21:23.390 --> 00:21:26.872
I'm always fascinated by people
that actually know people.

00:21:26.872 --> 00:21:29.210
Versus knowing of them.

00:21:29.210 --> 00:21:30.350
- [Tilman] Interesting.

00:21:30.350 --> 00:21:31.620
- You know my big game in life,

00:21:31.620 --> 00:21:33.060
there's plenty of people
with the way I roll,

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:36.440
the cursing, the bravado, the
confidence, the conviction,

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:38.490
especially if I'm putting
pressure on your business,

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:41.860
you're not gonna love me but I care about

00:21:41.860 --> 00:21:44.210
what the people that
actually know me say about me

00:21:44.210 --> 00:21:46.580
and in that group a couple
of them really know you

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:48.960
and are also very close to me

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:50.810
so they're not gonna bullshit me.

00:21:50.810 --> 00:21:51.643
It's really cool.

00:21:51.643 --> 00:21:52.500
I like that.

00:21:52.500 --> 00:21:54.480
- [Tilman] I appreciate that.

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:55.560
So keep going.

00:21:55.560 --> 00:21:57.590
- Well why did you write the book?

00:21:57.590 --> 00:21:59.150
- It's really interesting,

00:21:59.150 --> 00:22:01.449
about 18 months ago HarperCollins...

00:22:01.449 --> 00:22:02.830
- [Gary] My publisher.

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:04.350
- Called me and said,

00:22:04.350 --> 00:22:09.350
hey we watch your television
show "Billion Dollar Buyer",

00:22:09.630 --> 00:22:12.840
we see you on all the business shows

00:22:12.840 --> 00:22:15.510
and you always have these
Tilmanisms and one liners

00:22:15.510 --> 00:22:17.197
and we've read all
these articles about you

00:22:17.197 --> 00:22:18.300
and how you say,

00:22:18.300 --> 00:22:20.210
oh I can see a burnt out light bulb

00:22:20.210 --> 00:22:23.070
at one of my restaurants from 40,000 feet,

00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:23.940
and they said,

00:22:23.940 --> 00:22:26.140
we'd like you to write a book.

00:22:26.140 --> 00:22:27.310
Well I always thought somebody would say,

00:22:27.310 --> 00:22:29.000
we want you to write a life story book.

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:30.760
And I said I can't do that until I'm older

00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:31.960
'cause I'm still doing deals

00:22:31.960 --> 00:22:34.460
and I wanna rip some people's ass, okay?

00:22:34.460 --> 00:22:36.657
So they said no we want you to...

00:22:36.657 --> 00:22:38.380
- [Gary] Do you garage sale?

00:22:38.380 --> 00:22:39.213
- [Tilman] Sir?

00:22:39.213 --> 00:22:41.040
- Do you ever garage sale?

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:42.510
Do you roll up on a garage sale

00:22:42.510 --> 00:22:45.331
and try to buy something for a dollar?

00:22:45.331 --> 00:22:46.420
That's bazaar for you, right?

00:22:46.420 --> 00:22:49.200
- Not as bazaar as my garage sale.

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:51.660
- But did you?

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:53.050
- Absolutely when I was younger.

00:22:53.050 --> 00:22:53.950
- [Gary] I apologize for interrupting,

00:22:53.950 --> 00:22:55.220
but you said "rip somebody's ass,"

00:22:55.220 --> 00:22:56.053
and I'm like,

00:22:57.890 --> 00:22:58.723
I'll give you a story.

00:22:58.723 --> 00:23:00.280
I was at the National
Sport's Car Convention.

00:23:00.280 --> 00:23:01.620
I gotta talk to you about basketball cards

00:23:01.620 --> 00:23:02.483
before we leave,

00:23:03.505 --> 00:23:05.920
I see a humongous
arbitrage in sports cards

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:07.677
and you need to go all in.

00:23:07.677 --> 00:23:08.510
(laughter)

00:23:08.510 --> 00:23:09.820
I'm being dead serious with you.

00:23:09.820 --> 00:23:11.420
On the record.

00:23:11.420 --> 00:23:12.930
I was at the National
Sports Car Convention

00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:15.960
in Chicago in late July this year.

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:17.320
I walked over to some guy's table,

00:23:17.320 --> 00:23:18.690
bought a Wayne Gretzky rookie card.

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:20.900
Not in great condition for $220,

00:23:20.900 --> 00:23:22.400
I brought it back to my table,

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:23.800
priced it at $260,

00:23:23.800 --> 00:23:26.350
an hour later somebody came
over and bought it for $260.

00:23:26.350 --> 00:23:28.340
I swear on everything,

00:23:28.340 --> 00:23:31.617
that $40.00 arb meant more to me than all

00:23:31.617 --> 00:23:33.890
$50 trillion deals I make every day.

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:34.723
- [Tilman] Absolutely.

00:23:34.723 --> 00:23:36.110
'Cause that's what we do.

00:23:36.110 --> 00:23:38.336
That's like me making the
deal at Zara for all these

00:23:38.336 --> 00:23:39.620
jackets on a clothes line.

00:23:39.620 --> 00:23:40.660
- [Gary] That's why I ask
you about garage sales.

00:23:40.660 --> 00:23:41.580
So anyway the reason why you don't want to

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:43.850
write a book is you still
got a couple moves in you

00:23:43.850 --> 00:23:45.030
and you don't want to put it out there

00:23:45.030 --> 00:23:46.080
because that's how you're
gonna fuckin' do it.

00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:48.480
- [Tilman] Right but
so they just wanted it

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:51.150
and say talk about how you made it.

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:53.810
What were your philosophies,

00:23:53.810 --> 00:23:58.223
all your tilmanisms and the 95/5 rule.

00:23:59.582 --> 00:24:02.710
95% of everything we do is
right everyday in business,

00:24:02.710 --> 00:24:04.600
but look for the 5% you do wrong

00:24:04.600 --> 00:24:06.760
and that's what makes you
better than everybody else.

00:24:06.760 --> 00:24:08.660
There are no spare customers, okay?

00:24:08.660 --> 00:24:11.210
It's a competitive world in
every one of our businesses.

00:24:11.210 --> 00:24:13.623
Take no out of your damn vocabulary.

00:24:13.623 --> 00:24:16.730
You're on a business trip and it's 11:02,

00:24:16.730 --> 00:24:19.740
you hang up the phone on a call,

00:24:19.740 --> 00:24:21.770
and you called out to
order some breakfast,

00:24:21.770 --> 00:24:24.350
and they say I'm sorry sir we
don't serve breakfast anymore.

00:24:24.350 --> 00:24:26.290
I say that's okay no problem
I don't need a waffle

00:24:26.290 --> 00:24:28.000
or an eggs Benedict.

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:30.810
Just throw a couple eggs
in a skillet scrambled,

00:24:30.810 --> 00:24:32.330
however it's easiest for you to do.

00:24:32.330 --> 00:24:34.370
I'm sorry sir we don't serve breakfast.

00:24:34.370 --> 00:24:35.600
Why are you telling me no?

00:24:35.600 --> 00:24:37.880
I didn't ask you for anything special.

00:24:37.880 --> 00:24:39.440
You're gonna throw a burger in the pan.

00:24:39.440 --> 00:24:42.377
Just throw me a couple of
eggs and take care of me.

00:24:42.377 --> 00:24:45.330
You're in the hospitality business, okay?

00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:46.800
And you're telling me no.

00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:48.860
And everybody's in the
hospitality business

00:24:48.860 --> 00:24:49.930
no matter what they do.

00:24:49.930 --> 00:24:52.260
You take care of your customer, okay?

00:24:52.260 --> 00:24:54.000
You take care of your customers.

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:55.890
It's no different than we walk in,

00:24:55.890 --> 00:24:57.025
your people meet us, polite,

00:24:57.025 --> 00:24:59.270
put a welcome up on the screen,

00:24:59.270 --> 00:25:00.103
you come in here,

00:25:00.103 --> 00:25:02.651
everybody couldn't be can I
get you some water, Tilman?

00:25:02.651 --> 00:25:03.950
Can I do this for you?

00:25:03.950 --> 00:25:05.410
You're in the service business!

00:25:05.410 --> 00:25:07.320
So why do you want to be an ass

00:25:07.320 --> 00:25:10.270
and say no to somebody
when it's just as easy

00:25:10.270 --> 00:25:11.973
to say yes or just give an alternative.

00:25:11.973 --> 00:25:13.250
- [Gary] Tilman, because the company

00:25:13.250 --> 00:25:15.690
is being run by somebody who's not.

00:25:15.690 --> 00:25:16.790
'Cause these companies get big

00:25:16.790 --> 00:25:18.680
and they hire somebody who
fuckin' went to Harvard

00:25:18.680 --> 00:25:20.010
and fuckin' worked at Bain and McKinsey

00:25:20.010 --> 00:25:22.800
and they don't know what the
fuck they're talking about.

00:25:22.800 --> 00:25:23.660
That's why, Tilman.

00:25:23.660 --> 00:25:25.640
- [Tilman] I loved my McKinsey consultants

00:25:25.640 --> 00:25:26.960
when I see 'em.

00:25:26.960 --> 00:25:27.853
I just love 'em.

00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:32.257
It's so funny you said that
because I have in this book,

00:25:32.257 --> 00:25:35.620
"Consultants will consult your
ass right out of business."

00:25:35.620 --> 00:25:36.453
- [Gary] 100%.

00:25:36.453 --> 00:25:38.070
- And I don't hire consultants.

00:25:38.070 --> 00:25:38.903
- [Gary] You know why?

00:25:38.903 --> 00:25:40.220
Because they've never
fuckin' done anything.

00:25:40.220 --> 00:25:43.730
- [Tilman] Unless I need
a reporter or something,

00:25:43.730 --> 00:25:45.908
and I tell 'em what I want 'em to say,

00:25:45.908 --> 00:25:46.741
that's when I walk in.

00:25:46.741 --> 00:25:47.574
- That's like me telling Russell Westbrook

00:25:47.574 --> 00:25:48.407
what to fuckin' do.

00:25:48.407 --> 00:25:49.240
- [Tilman] 100%.

00:25:49.240 --> 00:25:50.170
- Stupid.

00:25:50.170 --> 00:25:51.470
I watch a lot of basketball.

00:25:51.470 --> 00:25:52.303
I've got ideas.

00:25:52.303 --> 00:25:53.710
It's fuckin' stupid.

00:25:53.710 --> 00:25:56.414
- 'Cause you know what you
know and what you don't know.

00:25:56.414 --> 00:25:58.200
- [Gary] It's unbelievable.

00:25:58.200 --> 00:25:59.980
It's why I love athletes.

00:25:59.980 --> 00:26:02.630
When you really get to know
them it's really fascinating

00:26:02.630 --> 00:26:04.220
how they talk about fans.

00:26:04.220 --> 00:26:05.620
Whatever variable.

00:26:05.620 --> 00:26:06.560
No matter how you take it,

00:26:06.560 --> 00:26:07.553
pro and con.

00:26:08.541 --> 00:26:10.540
If you melt every athlete,

00:26:10.540 --> 00:26:12.950
what they really say when
they talk about fans is,

00:26:12.950 --> 00:26:14.370
they just don't know.

00:26:14.370 --> 00:26:15.203
Whether they say they're assholes

00:26:15.203 --> 00:26:16.230
or whether they love them,

00:26:16.230 --> 00:26:18.980
they just understand that
they have no fuckin' idea.

00:26:18.980 --> 00:26:20.170
They have no context.

00:26:20.170 --> 00:26:21.080
It's actually my favorite thing

00:26:21.080 --> 00:26:22.080
that's going on on earth right now.

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:24.220
We live in a judgment society.

00:26:24.220 --> 00:26:26.040
Because it's social and
everybody has a voice

00:26:26.040 --> 00:26:28.370
and we've always been judgemental.

00:26:28.370 --> 00:26:29.750
That's how humans are.

00:26:29.750 --> 00:26:30.880
Small little town,

00:26:30.880 --> 00:26:33.330
somebody says oh Ruth's
borsch is not as good.

00:26:33.330 --> 00:26:35.510
Like, we've always been
judgemental, right?

00:26:35.510 --> 00:26:39.170
But now we can see the
physical manifestation of it.

00:26:39.170 --> 00:26:40.003
But the reality is,

00:26:40.003 --> 00:26:41.320
people are judging people left and right

00:26:41.320 --> 00:26:43.550
without knowing anything about the truth.

00:26:43.550 --> 00:26:45.141
Nobody knows you.

00:26:45.141 --> 00:26:46.350
- [Tilman] Nobody.

00:26:46.350 --> 00:26:49.490
And that's why when I have a good friend

00:26:49.490 --> 00:26:50.570
that'll say to me,

00:26:50.570 --> 00:26:52.280
I was over here and I was at a bar

00:26:52.280 --> 00:26:54.100
and they were trashing your ass,

00:26:54.100 --> 00:26:54.933
and I'll say to them,

00:26:54.933 --> 00:26:56.800
well do you know Tilman Fertitta?

00:26:56.800 --> 00:26:58.660
No but that's what I heard.

00:26:58.660 --> 00:27:02.039
Because people don't like people on top.

00:27:02.039 --> 00:27:03.469
And you're on top,

00:27:03.469 --> 00:27:05.150
I'm on top,

00:27:05.150 --> 00:27:07.550
and I do think people though

00:27:07.550 --> 00:27:09.210
are getting a little nicer though.

00:27:09.210 --> 00:27:11.240
I think people now seem to
respect you or whatever.

00:27:11.240 --> 00:27:15.450
- [Gary] Listen we have
macro anxiety in ecosystem

00:27:15.450 --> 00:27:16.970
and eventually that becomes tiring

00:27:16.970 --> 00:27:19.370
and people go the world
just ebbs and flows.

00:27:19.370 --> 00:27:20.720
It's not super complicated.

00:27:22.050 --> 00:27:24.890
Tilman so I like this story,

00:27:24.890 --> 00:27:26.100
'cause I wanna go back
'cause I like learning

00:27:26.100 --> 00:27:28.757
from storytelling and you're
doing a great job at it.

00:27:28.757 --> 00:27:30.280
You don't have to pay
back for an extra five,

00:27:30.280 --> 00:27:31.290
that gives you ammo,

00:27:31.290 --> 00:27:33.251
you have all these great brands,

00:27:33.251 --> 00:27:35.610
so I'm really excited to dig
in here for a little bit.

00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:37.130
By the way my entire company,

00:27:37.130 --> 00:27:38.300
which we're a global marketing company,

00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:39.200
a thousand people,

00:27:39.200 --> 00:27:40.033
big business,

00:27:40.033 --> 00:27:41.490
it's all a front.

00:27:41.490 --> 00:27:43.350
I'm building all this infrastructure

00:27:43.350 --> 00:27:45.270
to do what you and I do at scale,

00:27:45.270 --> 00:27:46.795
which is when the economy collapses,

00:27:46.795 --> 00:27:48.740
I'm gonna buy historic brands

00:27:48.740 --> 00:27:49.817
and refurbish them.

00:27:49.817 --> 00:27:51.110
- [Tilman] And that's what I do.

00:27:51.110 --> 00:27:53.301
And I was the first person out there

00:27:53.301 --> 00:27:55.937
to start a platform in saying,

00:27:57.040 --> 00:27:57.990
let me give you an example.

00:27:57.990 --> 00:28:00.320
So I go public in 1993.

00:28:00.320 --> 00:28:01.153
- [Gary] With what?

00:28:01.153 --> 00:28:02.230
- [Tilman] With 10 restaurants.

00:28:02.230 --> 00:28:03.063
- Which ones?

00:28:03.063 --> 00:28:03.970
- [Tilman] Landry's Seafood House.

00:28:03.970 --> 00:28:06.470
I'm making $6 million a year in EBITDA

00:28:06.470 --> 00:28:08.080
and all of the sudden you wake up

00:28:08.080 --> 00:28:11.250
and you're on the Nasdaq and
you're worth $100 million.

00:28:11.250 --> 00:28:14.030
So over the next few
years I built restaurants.

00:28:14.030 --> 00:28:17.922
Then we hit a dip in the late 90's.

00:28:17.922 --> 00:28:18.755
- [Gary] Why?

00:28:18.755 --> 00:28:20.019
- The tech dip.

00:28:20.019 --> 00:28:21.020
- [Gary] You mean the market?

00:28:21.020 --> 00:28:21.980
- [Tilman] The market did.

00:28:21.980 --> 00:28:23.970
- [Gary] Your stock price went down.

00:28:23.970 --> 00:28:24.803
- Yes.

00:28:24.803 --> 00:28:25.664
- [Gary] I understand.

00:28:25.664 --> 00:28:27.210
Was your business healthy though?

00:28:27.210 --> 00:28:28.220
- [Tilman] My business was healthy

00:28:28.220 --> 00:28:29.470
and you know this,

00:28:29.470 --> 00:28:31.240
what your stock does...

00:28:31.240 --> 00:28:32.470
- Has nothing to do, nothing.

00:28:32.470 --> 00:28:34.163
- [Tilman] Has nothing
to do with each other.

00:28:35.147 --> 00:28:36.393
- Can I ask a question?

00:28:36.393 --> 00:28:37.226
Tilman wait a minute.

00:28:37.226 --> 00:28:38.180
This is actually
completely confusing to me.

00:28:38.180 --> 00:28:40.200
You were a public CEO?

00:28:40.200 --> 00:28:41.033
- [Tilman] Yes.

00:28:41.033 --> 00:28:41.866
For 18 years.

00:28:41.866 --> 00:28:43.350
- How the fuck did you
deal with that shit?

00:28:43.350 --> 00:28:46.312
- Because I performed and...

00:28:46.312 --> 00:28:48.580
- [Gary] You told them
to go fuck themselves?

00:28:48.580 --> 00:28:50.690
You had to a little bit, right?

00:28:50.690 --> 00:28:53.340
You had to tell these
people on the earning call

00:28:53.340 --> 00:28:56.055
who would try to ticky
tack you on some bullshit.

00:28:56.055 --> 00:28:58.230
- [Tilman] The longer I
got and the richer I got

00:28:58.230 --> 00:29:00.095
I put up with less, okay?

00:29:00.095 --> 00:29:01.195
You got to remember...

00:29:02.080 --> 00:29:02.913
- [Gary] Leverage is a funny thing.

00:29:02.913 --> 00:29:06.140
- [Tilman] No but it's
interesting because...

00:29:06.140 --> 00:29:09.010
- You had a board that was all in on you?

00:29:09.010 --> 00:29:09.970
- [Tilman] Let me,

00:29:09.970 --> 00:29:13.615
first off when I took
it public I owned 100%.

00:29:13.615 --> 00:29:16.040
- [Gary] That's an easy way to start.

00:29:16.040 --> 00:29:18.279
- So it was my board.

00:29:18.279 --> 00:29:21.323
I had a board but it was my board.

00:29:22.320 --> 00:29:25.220
And if you keep over 50%,

00:29:25.220 --> 00:29:27.250
you only have to have an
outside audit committee.

00:29:27.250 --> 00:29:28.890
You don't even have to
have an outside board.

00:29:28.890 --> 00:29:29.723
So you don't have to have a

00:29:29.723 --> 00:29:31.130
compensation committee and all that.

00:29:31.130 --> 00:29:32.020
- [Gary] I understand.

00:29:32.020 --> 00:29:34.260
- So I basically
controlled the company for

00:29:34.260 --> 00:29:36.680
many many years but I
grew it from a $30 million

00:29:36.680 --> 00:29:39.550
company to when I took it private,

00:29:39.550 --> 00:29:43.050
being an opportunist x
amount of years later,

00:29:43.050 --> 00:29:45.000
to a multi billion dollar company.

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:48.110
But we got off track somewhere.

00:29:48.110 --> 00:29:50.300
But what happened is I realized that

00:29:50.300 --> 00:29:55.300
I could go buy somebody
and get rid of their GNA

00:29:55.900 --> 00:29:57.090
and bring them to Houston

00:29:57.090 --> 00:29:58.400
and keep the store operations

00:29:58.400 --> 00:29:59.960
but not have all that overhead.

00:29:59.960 --> 00:30:00.976
And so over the next...

00:30:00.976 --> 00:30:01.809
- [Gary] That was arbitrage.

00:30:01.809 --> 00:30:05.520
- Because over the next
20 years what I did was,

00:30:05.520 --> 00:30:07.730
and I can tell you every
city that I shut down

00:30:07.730 --> 00:30:10.270
somebody from Portland,
McCormick, &amp; Schmidt

00:30:10.270 --> 00:30:14.880
to Chicago Chart House's
home offices were there.

00:30:14.880 --> 00:30:17.320
And Martin's home offices were there.

00:30:17.320 --> 00:30:21.300
And Laguna Beach, it was
Bubba Gump and Claim Jumper.

00:30:21.300 --> 00:30:22.850
And I can just go from city to city.

00:30:22.850 --> 00:30:25.240
Minneapolis, it was
Oceanaire and Rainforest.

00:30:25.240 --> 00:30:26.970
So I bought all these companies

00:30:26.970 --> 00:30:28.460
and I just shut their office down

00:30:28.460 --> 00:30:30.980
and consolidate in Houston

00:30:30.980 --> 00:30:34.170
and save millions and millions on GNA.

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:35.987
And so I built this platform

00:30:35.987 --> 00:30:38.090
and I bought all these companies, right?

00:30:38.090 --> 00:30:41.310
And rejuvenated them if they needed it.

00:30:41.310 --> 00:30:42.860
Some of them weren't even screwed up.

00:30:42.860 --> 00:30:44.890
They were just screwed
up public companies.

00:30:44.890 --> 00:30:48.597
But I bought Rainforest for $75 million

00:30:48.597 --> 00:30:50.253
and it was making $30 million a year

00:30:50.253 --> 00:30:52.470
and $15 million in the bank.

00:30:52.470 --> 00:30:53.730
But their stock had fallen

00:30:53.730 --> 00:30:55.340
'cause their gross slowed down.

00:30:55.340 --> 00:30:57.040
But it was a great company to buy.

00:30:57.040 --> 00:30:59.220
And back then everybody else was scared.

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:00.680
Private equity hadn't gotten into

00:31:00.680 --> 00:31:02.330
consumer businesses yet

00:31:02.330 --> 00:31:04.620
and people that are
strategic wouldn't buy 'em

00:31:04.620 --> 00:31:07.140
